# The "Daily Driver" Checklist (Top 20%)

These are the concepts you'll use in almost every professional C# backend project. Mastering them first gives you the biggest return on your time:

* ✅ Variables, types, operators
* ✅ Control flow
* ✅ Strings
* ✅ Arrays
* ✅ `List<T>`, `Dictionary<TKey,TValue>`, `HashSet<T>`
* ✅ LINQ
* ✅ Classes, interfaces, records
* ✅ Exceptions
* ✅ Generics
* ✅ Delegates (`Func`, `Action`, `Predicate`)
* ✅ `async` / `await` and `Task`
* ✅ Memory basics (`IDisposable`, `using`, GC fundamentals)
* ✅ File and JSON APIs
* ✅ `HttpClient`
* ✅ Dependency Injection
* ✅ ASP.NET Core Web APIs
* ✅ Entity Framework Core
* ✅ Authentication & Authorization
* ✅ Unit testing
* ✅ Logging and configuration

---

## One improvement I'd make to this roadmap

Rather than simply reading about each phase, **build a small project after every 2–3 phases**. For example:

* After Phases 1–6: Console-based Student Management System
* After Phases 7–10: Library or Inventory Management app using LINQ and OOP
* After Phases 11–16: Console REST client that consumes a public API and stores JSON
* After Phases 17–22: Full ASP.NET Core Web API with EF Core, JWT authentication, and SQL Server/PostgreSQL
* After Phases 23–24: Production-style microservice using Docker, Redis, RabbitMQ/Kafka, logging, health checks, and OpenTelemetry

This project-based approach helps the concepts stick and mirrors how you'll actually use them in professional backend development.
